3. `done`'s GUARD — TESTED DIRECTLY. The load-bearing claim is TRUE.
--------------------------------------------------------------------
packages/commands/src/lifecycle.ts:195-214, exercised through the real CLI
with the manifest declaring `tests` kind: automated, opus at state building.
Five record shapes, each written then `bisellium done` run:
  A  gate absent from the record entirely
       -> "W-001: gates not passed: tests"   exit 1, state unchanged
  B  status: passed, certifies: "dirty:abc"
       -> "gates not passed: tests"          exit 1, state unchanged
  C  status: waived, waived_by: patron
       -> "gates not passed: tests"          exit 1, state unchanged
  D  status: failed, certifies: "tree:deadbeef"
       -> "gates not passed: tests"          exit 1, state unchanged
  E  status: passed, certifies: "tree:deadbeef" (an ARBITRARY hash)
       -> "W-001: done"                      exit 0
Case E is the one hole in `done` alone — it checks the `tree:` PREFIX, never
that the hash matches. merge closes it: staleCertificateError compares the
recorded hash against the branch's current source tree, which is exactly
what lab8 above demonstrates. The two together form a complete chain:
  `done` demands every declared automated gate have a tree: certificate;
  `merge` demands state: done ON THE BRANCH;
  `merge` demands every recorded tree: certificate match the branch's tree.
I could not construct a CLI-reachable path to `state: done` with an
uncertified automated gate. lifecycle.ts:219 is the only writer of
`state: done` in the codebase (grepped setIn(["state"]) across packages/,
apps/, adapters/ — writes.ts:506 and :621 are petitio and greenlight
states, lifecycle.ts:338 is review --fail dropping back to building). The
builder's argument for dropping the rule holds. Verified, not accepted.

