names the ref and the remedy — the round-6 complaint is answered.

ON THE DESIGN CALL (B6.3) — I WAS WRONG AT ROUND 6, AND M-S1 PROVES IT
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
I suggested splitting provenance: certificates from the branch, state from
the checkout. The builder refused and is right. M-S1 implements my
suggestion and 29 catches it in both directions, but the deeper reason is
structural: dropping the absence rule makes the fail-open guarantee rest
ENTIRELY on `done`'s gate (section 3), and that guarantee only transfers to
`merge` because `merge` reads `state` from the same ref it reads the
certificates from. Split them and a `done` run against the TRUNK's copy —
which on this very PR carries passed gates certifying the stale round-4 tree
— would authorise a merge of a branch record that has no certificates at
all. My round-6 suggestion would have reopened the exact hole the absence
rule was there to plug, at the same moment the rule was removed. One
provenance, one rule, is correct. Endorsed without reservation.

B7.1 (BLOCKING) — sourceExcludeDirs is the LAST mixed-provenance read, and
it produces an unclearable loop with a remedy that provably cannot clear.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
`readOpusRecord` now reads the record from `branch`'s ref. `sourceExcludeDirs`
(branch.ts:81-89), which supplies the exclude set that DEFINES the hash the
record is compared against, still reads the manifest off DISK — whatever
`--studio` shows in the checkout `merge` runs from. `verify`, run on the
branch, uses the BRANCH's manifest. So an opus whose own work touches
`source_excludes` is certified under one exclude set and compared under
another.

  lab16, real CLI throughout. The opus's work: add `vendor/` and declare
  `source_excludes: [vendor/]` in studio/bisellium.yml, committed on the
  branch. Then on the branch: verify -> certifies tree:22d520cb (vendor/
  excluded); done; committed. merge from the trunk:
    "opus/W-001's tree no longer matches its certificate — ... certifies
     tree:22d520cb..., which no longer matches its current tree
     (tree:050362e1...); check out opus/W-001, re-run "bisellium verify
     W-001" there, commit the result on opus/W-001, then switch back to
     master and retry the merge"                                     exit 1
  Follow that remedy verbatim, on the branch:
    bisellium verify W-001 ...  -> "tests: passed ci/W-001-tests-22d520cb.log"
    git commit                  -> "nothing to commit, working tree clean"
                                   (the certificate is byte-identical — it
                                    must be: verify is computing the right
                                    hash, merge is computing the wrong one)
    bisellium merge W-001 ...   -> the IDENTICAL message               exit 1
  The loop does not terminate. This is the same shape as round-6 B6.1, and
  it is not cleared by a rebase either: the manifest change is the BRANCH's
  own commit, so replaying it onto the trunk leaves the divergence intact.
  The only escape is to run `merge` from a checkout sitting on the branch —
  where it then half-succeeds: "master fast-forwarded to opus/W-001, but
  could not delete opus/W-001: ... used by worktree", exit 1, code landed,
  branch orphaned. Nothing documents either.

  I graded the sibling of this finding A6.2 (advisory) at round 6 because I
  tested the GATE-LIST case, which a rebase does clear. I did not test the
  exclude-set case. That was my error, and this is the worse half: the
  exclude set feeds the hash itself, and nothing clears it.

  The fix is the rule the builder already established and already owns:
  everything `merge` compares comes from `branch`'s ref. `readOpusRecord`
  already has the `git show <branch>:<studio-rel>/...` mechanism and
  `studioRelToRepo` already computes the path; `sourceExcludeDirs` needs to
  use them (and `resolveIntegration` should be judged the same way — the
  trunk's strategy, less severe, untested here). After that, branch.ts has
  no disk read left in the compare path at all, which is the invariant this
  opus has been converging on for three rounds.

B7.2 (BLOCKING) — docs/ADOPTION.md documents a refusal this same commit
deleted, and the refusal does not happen.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
The A6.1 correction rewrote the `dirty:` sentence from "is never compared
here" to its opposite:
  "A `certifies: dirty:<hash>` gate ... IS compared here in one direction:
   `merge` demands a `tree:` certificate specifically, so a gate that only
   ever certified `dirty:<hash>` is refused with "no recorded tree:
   certificate" — a `dirty:` certificate never satisfies a merge, by design"
   (docs/ADOPTION.md:236-240)
That refusal WAS the absence rule, removed 100 lines earlier in the same
commit. `grep -rn "no recorded tree" packages/ docs/` returns the doc line
and one test NAME — the string exists in no source file. staleCertificateError
now skips any gate whose `certifies` does not start with `tree:`, so a
`dirty:` certificate is not examined at all.
  lab12, real CLI: verify --allow-dirty on the branch -> `certifies:
  dirty:22d520cb`, status passed. `bisellium done` correctly refuses it
  ("gates not passed: tests"). Forcing state: done in the record and
  committing it, then merging from the trunk:
    "merged opus/W-001 into master"                                  exit 0
  i.e. merge lands it silently. The doc claims the opposite, in the
  paragraph that is the written contract for the mechanism under review.
The underlying behaviour is defensible (the `done` gate is what refuses a
dirty certificate, and it does). What is not defensible is the contract
document asserting a safety property that was deleted in the same commit
that asserted it. Either restore the sentence to the truth ("a `dirty:`
certificate is never compared by merge; `bisellium done` is what refuses
one") or make the code match the doc. This is one paragraph.

Advisories
----------
A7.1  "per P-005" on behaviour 28 (brief line 112, branch.test.ts:953,
      branch.ts's B6.1 comment) is the wrong citation — P-005 is the
      assertion-level-reds petitio. The retire-in-place practice is right;
      its actual justification is countBehaviours' contiguous 1..N read in
      rules/evidence.ts. Cite that, or open a petitio for the practice.
A7.2  The B6.3 remedy's step 1 aborts in the case the message is written
      FOR. lab63c: operator on the trunk runs `bisellium done W-026`
      (exactly CLAUDE.md's documented invocation) — it succeeds, because
      master's copy of W-026.md is `state: building` with passed gates
      certifying the stale tree:5ab03a0b, and `done` checks only the
      `tree:` prefix. merge then refuses and says "run done on opus/W-026
      and commit it there"; `git checkout opus/W-026` refuses, because
      `done` itself just dirtied the trunk (studio/opera/W-026.md AND
      studio/.bisellium/events.jsonl). Not a loop — git's own message names
      the escape and a stash clears it — so advisory, not blocking. But it
      will fire on THIS PR the moment it is marked done. The real fix is in
      `done`, not `merge`: refuse (or warn) when run on the trunk for an
      opus that has a live branch. Worth its own opus.
      lab63b: if the operator COMMITS that trunk-side `done` instead, the
      branch's own `done` then conflicts on rebase — "could not be rebased
      onto master — resolve the conflict on opus/W-026 and retry", which is
      honest and recoverable.
A7.3  `bisellium done` refuses a WAIVED automated gate (section 3 case C:
      lifecycle.ts:205 demands status === "passed" for kind: automated). So
      the round-6 lab11 shape is unreachable through the CLI in the first
      place, and a Patron waiver on an automated gate — a first-class
      ProbatioStatus that check.ts:487/495/509 and the web gate ladder both
      treat as satisfied — cannot be honoured anywhere in the lifecycle.
      Out of W-026's scope; the defect is in `done`, and removing the
      absence rule was still the right call for merge. File it.
A7.4  Behaviour 29(b)'s first two assertions (`result.ok === false` and
      `.includes("opus/W-103")`) are satisfied by ANY refusal naming the
      branch — under M-S1 they pass on "branch opus/W-103 has diverged from
      master — rebase first". Only the third assertion bites. It does bite,
      so the behaviour is not vacuous; tighten the first two anyway.
A7.5  29 and 30 are labelled "pre-implementation" in the brief but are
      partial reds (one new assertion each; section 6). The LOGS are honest
      — they show the PASS lines. The brief should carry the same
      qualifier, as it does for 22's "(mutation-record)".
A7.6  Carried and still accepted: A6.6 (prune.ts:49 and close.ts:22 read
      the opus record off disk; `git branch --merged HEAD` is prune's real
      guard, so a wrong read can only keep a branch, never land code),
      A4.1 (pull_after_push inert under pr.required, documented), A4.5 (a
      refused merge leaves the branch rebased, benign under
      force-with-lease).

On the design question the brief asks
--------------------------------------
The remaining defect is in the IMPLEMENTATION, not in the design of putting
certificate checking inside `merge`.

I say that with more confidence this round than last, because the design
just passed the test that matters: its belt-and-braces rule was removed
entirely and the fail-open lab still refuses (section 1), because the
guarantee genuinely lives in `done` + the branch-ref read together (section
3). A mechanism that survives having one of its two refusal paths deleted is
a mechanism that was load-bearing in the right place.

Every blocking finding in rounds 5, 6 and 7 is one bug: a read pointing at
the checkout instead of the branch ref. B5.1 was the opus record. B6.3 was
`state` inside that record — same read, already fixed, argued correctly.
B7.1 is `sourceExcludeDirs`, the last one, and the one that defines the hash
everything else is compared against. That is a converging sequence, not a
design that keeps failing: the rule "everything merge compares comes from
`branch`'s ref" is correct, and the implementation is one function short of
obeying it.

Verdict
-------
FAIL, round 7 — but on two findings that are one function and one paragraph,
with no design change and no new behaviour required beyond a red on the
exclude-set loop.

What this round genuinely closed, all of it executed: the fail-open lab
refuses with the absence rule gone; the waived gate merges; `done`'s guard
is real and I tested all five shapes against the CLI; both remedies work
verbatim with the real commands from a clean trunk; the state path is pinned
by 29 and my own M-S1/M-S2; 28's flip is pinned by M-A2 and is honestly
labelled; the reds reproduce exactly against the previous implementation;
and the gates are honest — tree:c56a5125 is what I recomputed for 73ad20d.

What blocks: an opus that touches `source_excludes` cannot be merged at all,
and the refusal's own remedy provably loops (B7.1, lab16); and ADOPTION.md
now promises a refusal the same commit deleted, which lab12 disproves
(B7.2). I graded B7.1's sibling advisory at round 6 on incomplete evidence;
that was my error and I am naming it rather than presenting this as new.

